# {{.project_name}}

Production-shaped Go HTTP API generated with [smokeforge](https://github.com/BoatengPeter/smokeforge).

## Stack

- Go 1.26 with Gin
- Layered architecture: transport, application service, and infrastructure seams
- Spec-first OpenAPI with `oapi-codegen`
- Swagger UI at `/docs` and the merged contract at `/openapi.json`
- RFC 9457 error responses
- `caarlos0/env` configuration with local `godotenv`
- Zap structured logging
- `go-playground/validator` request validation
- Per-IP token-bucket rate limiting
- UUIDv7 identifiers
- Optional PostgreSQL with pgx, sqlc, and goose
- Optional Redis cache with fail-open JSON helpers
- Optional JWT auth with rotating refresh sessions and TOTP
- Optional Docker and GitHub Actions overlays
- Project-scoped Go agent skills

## Quick start

The generator writes `.env` from `.env.example`. If you are running from a
checkout without that file, copy it manually.

```bash
make install
make run
```

The API listens on <http://localhost:{{.http_port}}>. Useful endpoints:

| Endpoint | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /healthz` | Process liveness |
| `GET /readyz` | Dependency readiness |
| `GET /api/v1/ping` | Application wiring check |
| `GET /docs` | Swagger UI |
| `GET /openapi.json` | Merged OpenAPI contract |

Swagger UI is enabled in development. Set `API_DOCS_ENABLED=true` to expose it
in production, or `false` to remove it everywhere.

## Architecture

This is the **DDD** variant. The ping behavior lives in its own bounded context
with domain and application layers.

```text
cmd/api/                      process startup and shutdown
internal/httpapi/             Gin transport, middleware, OpenAPI, Swagger
internal/response/    Success responses and RFC 9457 error responses
internal/app/                 facade adapting the bounded context to HTTP
internal/ping/domain/         pure ping domain value
internal/ping/application/    ping use case handler
internal/config/              typed environment configuration
internal/logging/             Zap logger construction
internal/server/              HTTP and pprof lifecycle, graceful shutdown
internal/version/             build metadata
```

Feature overlays add self-contained packages such as `internal/database` and
`internal/auth`. Auth keeps its application core free of transport and
infrastructure imports; HTTP, PostgreSQL, email, and Google OAuth live in
`internal/auth/http`, `internal/auth/postgres`, `internal/auth/email`, and
`internal/auth/google`. Features register routes through `httpapi.Registrar`
and contribute their own OpenAPI documents; the base merges them at startup.

